(project borge-docs-architecture
 (section program-architecture
  (doc "How borge is structured: one root spec, inlined sub-specs, no orphans")
  (status implemented)
  (details
   (doc (|
        The root borge.borg file is the architectural contract. It
        declares what modules exist and how they relate by inlining
        their specs. It is the AGENTS.md equivalent for borge projects.

        (convention ocaml-dune) declares the structural rules for this
        project. Borge loads the rule set and uses it for structural drift
        detection. The convention covers: lib/ owns domain logic, bin/ owns
        CLI IO, dune files define libraries and executables, public_name
        conventions, test/ structure. Other conventions: go-standard,
        c-makefile, etc. This way lib.borg describes WHAT the lib does,
        not HOW OCaml works — the convention handles the how.

        Every .borg file must either be inlined by a parent or declare
        no-inline itself. A .borg file with no parent and no no-inline
        declaration is orphaned — this is structural drift.

        The inline directive is by filename/path and supports two target
        formats (with or without quotes; quotes allow '/' subdirectories):
          (inline cli.borg)
          (inline "lib/core/core.borg")
          (inline login.mli)
        The first form targets a .borg file containing the spec. The
        quoted form is the same but may include a relative path. The
        .mli form targets an .mli file; the spec lives in a
        (* borge (subsection ...) *) comment at the top of the .mli.
        The parent owns the architectural relationship. The child owns
        the detail.

        Drift detection covers three layers:
          1. Spec drift: .borg file describes something not in the code
          2. Code drift: code implements something not in .borg
          3. Structural drift: code organization violates the convention
             (e.g. domain logic in bin/ when ocaml-dune says lib/ owns it)
      |))
  )
  (subsection spec-interface-implementation-layers
   (doc "The .borg → .mli → .ml layering borge is aware of")
   (status planned)
   (details
    (doc (|
         Borge's project hierarchy has three layers, and borge is
         aware of each at the level appropriate to it:

           .borg   — intent. Borge OWNS this layer: reads, writes,
                     validates, formats it. The spec is primary.
           .mli    — interface contract. Borge is AWARE of this layer:
                     reads it for drift checks, never restates it in
                     the spec. The .mli is the integration contract
                     between subtrees — what one module owes another.
           .ml     — implementation. Borge CHECKS this layer against
                     both .borg (does it match the intent?) and .mli
                     (does it match the interface?). The .ml is the
                     disposable, regenerable artifact.

         The inline tree (root .borg inlines child .borg files) is the
         top of this hierarchy. Below it, each library's .borg is aware
         of the .mli files in its scope and reads them as drift inputs.
         Borge does not duplicate .mli content into .borg — the .mli
         is the authoritative interface; the .borg is the authoritative
         intent; restating either in the other creates drift by design.

         Formal requirement (planned): every dune library has a .borg
         file that is inlined by the root, and each such .borg is
         aware of the .mli files in its scope. A library with no .borg,
         or a .borg that ignores its .mli files, is structural drift.
         This keeps the loose tree loose (per-library .borg, writable
         by humans) while making the coverage complete.
       |))
   )
  )
 )
